Alice Josephine Davis — known to her family and friends throughout her life as Jo — was born in 1904 in the small mountain town of Alma, Park County, Colorado, the daughter of Robert Davis and Anna Maud Woodruff, and so a granddaughter, on her mother's side, of John Farney Woodruff and Annie E. Chaney. Her childhood moved through several of Colorado's mining towns: the 1910 census places the family in Florissant, Teller County, and a postcard her mother sent home in December 1913 puts them near St. Elmo, in the Chalk Creek country outside Buena Vista. By 1918, when Alice Josephine was fourteen, the family had moved on again, this time to Mineral County, settling in Creede — the same year her teenage brother, Robert Lincoln Davis, died in the influenza pandemic. Two years later, when Alice Josephine was sixteen, her parents' marriage ended in divorce, filed in that same county; her father left Colorado for Missouri not long after, and her mother remarried within weeks.

Alice Josephine herself married in 1921, at seventeen — a quiet wedding on Tuesday, July 5th, at her mother's home, performed by Judge Butler. Her husband, William Thomas Jackson, was a Creede-raised boy known to everyone as Billy; the local paper, reporting the marriage under the headline "Billy and Joe," noted that the bride was known to her own friends as "Joe." The couple remained in Creede, raising four children through the 1920s and 1930s — Joel, William Charles ("Scoot"), John Richard, and a daughter still living today. It was a marriage that, in its own quiet way, knit together the family's two Mineral County threads: her own parents had divorced in that same county the year before, and Billy's family had mined and raised children in its camps for a generation before him.

Jo loved reading, arrowhead hunting, and fishing, and kept an impressive arrowhead collection built up over years in the hills around Creede. She was known for her wicked skill at cards and, above all, for a kind and gentle nature that made her home a place friends and family of all ages felt free to simply drop in on, certain of a hot cup of coffee and someone glad to listen. She lived out her entire life in Creede, with one exception: in 1980, dying of cancer, she was hospitalized in Colorado Springs, where she died — not a relocation, but simply the place her final medical care took her, more than sixty years after she'd first arrived in Mineral County as a girl of fourteen.

<h3>Gravesite Details</h3>

Jo was cremated and, at her request, her ashes were placed in her brother Bob's grave. Her children erected a headstone for their parents and moved Jo's ashes to this site. Her husband, Billy Jackson, is buried in the Prescott National Cemetery.